716"} "Willy Wang"] " of the Philippines did compete, as did 2005 nangun World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/902693"} "Pui Fook Chien"] " of Malaysia. The nanquan event was contested closely, with five competitors rated between 9.74 and 9.70. Wang won both disciplines however, to earn first place before " [:a {:href "/athletes/902690"} "Peng Wei-Chua"] " and Pui. It was only the second time a Philippine athlete won first place at the Olympics, neither time in a full-medal event. The first time had been at the " [:a {:href "/results/900492"} "1988 women’s ten-pin bowling demonstration"] " in Seoul."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Nanquan"] [:th {} "Nangun"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902716"} "Willy Wang"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/PHI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/PHI.png"}] "PHI"]] [:td {} "19.44"] [:td {} "9.74 "] [:td {} "9.70 "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902690"} "Peng Wei-Chua"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TPE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TPE.png"}] "TPE"]] [:td {} "19.39"] [:td {} "9.70 "] [:td {} "9.69 "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902693"} "Pui Fook Chien"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/MAS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/MAS.png"}] "MAS"]] [:td {} "19.34"] [:td {} "9.72 "] [:td {} "9.62 "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902657"} "Koki Nakata"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/count